--TEST--
Forgetting a name nothing was stored under is neither an error nor an answer, so forgetting twice reaches the state forgetting once reaches, and one name goes without its neighbours — `rule:concurrency/cross-request-state-is-explicit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
var $cache = Core\Cache::local();

// § 1: an entry may be absent at any time, for any reason. A name nothing wrote
// is therefore already forgotten, and there is nothing here for a `try` to
// catch — `forget` reports no more about what was there than `get` does.
$cache->forget("nothing-wrote-this");
echo "a name nothing wrote: ", $cache->get("nothing-wrote-this") == null ? "null" : "something", "\n";

// The second call reaches the state the first reached, which is what lets a
// caller repeat the operation without first asking what happened.
$cache->put("twice", "written");
$cache->forget("twice");
var $once = $cache->get("twice") == null;
$cache->forget("twice");
var $twice = $cache->get("twice") == null;
echo "forgetting twice: ", $once && $twice ? "the state forgetting once reaches" : "two states", "\n";

// Counted over a whole table rather than read off one line: a store that forgot
// the name it was handed and the one beside it would still print both rows
// above.
array<string> $keys = ["alpha", "beta", "gamma"];
foreach ($keys as string $key) {
    $cache->put($key, "<" . $key . ">");
}
$cache->forget("beta");
int $left = 0;
foreach ($keys as string $key) {
    if ($cache->get($key) != null) {
        $left = $left + 1;
    }
}
echo "one name of ", Core\Arr::count($keys), " forgotten: ", $left, " left\n";
--EXPECT--
a name nothing wrote: null
forgetting twice: the state forgetting once reaches
one name of 3 forgotten: 2 left
